{"step_index":8,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-08T05:02:50Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-08T10:32:50+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-08T10:32:50+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/Tier1_EasyWins_CheatSheet.md`\nTotal Lines: 175\nTotal Bytes: 10224\nShowing lines 1 to 175\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # GTU Mid-Sem Master Cheat Sheet: Tier 1 & Easy Wins (UPDATED)\n2: \n3: This document contains exactly what you must write to get full marks for the highest-probability topics, including all necessary diagrams, advantages, and limitations required for 7-mark GTU questions.\n4: \n5: ---\n6: \n7: ## CHAPTER 1: FOUNDRY TECHNOLOGY\n8: \n9: ### 1. Six Applications of Casting (3 Marks)\n10: *Easy Win (Appeared in actual mid-sem)*\n11: **Write these exact 6 points:**\n12: 1. Automotive engine blocks and cylinder heads.\n13: 2. Machine tool beds and frames (due to damping capacity).\n14: 3. Turbine blades (aerospace).\n15: 4. Railway wheels and tracks.\n16: 5. Pump housings and valve bodies.\n17: 6. Agricultural machinery components.\n18: \n19: ### 2. Pattern Allowances - Theory (7 Marks)\n20: *Tier 1*\n21: **What to write for full marks:**\n22: *   **Definition:** Intentional dimensional modifications made to a pattern to compensate for metal shrinkage, machining, and casting defects.\n23: *   **1. Shrinkage Allowance:** Metals shrink when cooling from liquid to solid. Pattern is made larger. (Values: CI = 10mm/m, Steel = 20mm/m, Al = 13mm/m).\n24: *   **2. Machining/Finish Allowance:** Extra material added to surfaces that require machining later. (Usually 1.5 to 6 mm).\n25: *   **3. Draft Allowance:** Taper applied to vertical faces of the pattern for easy withdrawal without damaging the mould. (1° to 3° external, 2° to 5° internal).\n26: *   **4. Shake/Rapping Allowance:** When a pattern is shaken to remove it, the mould cavity enlarges. Pattern is made *slightly smaller* to compensate. (Negative allowance).\n27: *   **5. Distortion/Camber Allowance:** Applied to U or V-shaped castings that tend to warp during cooling. Pattern is bent in the opposite direction.\n28: *   **Diagram Required (Crucial for 7M):** Draw a 2D cross-section of a solid block. Show a dotted line outside for \"Machining Allowance\" and angled vertical sides for \"Draft Allowance\".\n29: \n30: ### 3. Pattern Allowance - Numerical (7 Marks)\n31: *Tier 1 (From actual mid-sem)*\n32: **How to score full marks:**\n33: 1.  **Read dimensions:** Identify L, W, H of the final casting.\n34: 2.  **Add Shrinkage:** Add the % or mm/m shrinkage to *each* dimension. \n35:     *   *Formula:* $Dimension_{new} = Dimension_{old} + (Dimension_{old} \\times \\frac{Shrinkage\\%}{100})$\n36: 3.  **Add Machining:** Add machining allowance to *every face that needs it*. \n37:     *   *Rule:* For a cube, machining of 2mm/side means adding 4mm to length, 4mm to width, 4mm to height (2 sides per dimension).\n38: 4.  **Add Draft:** Applied to vertical dimensions. Add the taper value.\n39: \n40: ### 4. Types of Patterns (3-4 Marks)\n41: *Tier 1*\n42: **List these and draw rough sketches for 3 of them:**\n43: 1. Single piece / Solid pattern (Sketch: Simple block)\n44: 2. Split / Two-piece pattern (Sketch: Two halves aligned with dowel pins)\n45: 3. Match plate pattern (Sketch: Central plate with pattern halves mounted on both sides)\n46: 4. Cope and drag pattern\n47: 5. Gated pattern (Sketch: Multiple patterns connected by a central runner/gate)\n48: 6. Sweep pattern (Sketch: Central vertical spindle with a sweeping wooden board)\n49: 7. Skeleton pattern\n50: 8. Loose piece pattern\n51: \n52: ### 5. Investment Casting (7 Marks)\n53: *Tier 1*\n54: **What to write for full marks:**\n55: 1.  **Principle:** Uses a wax pattern destroyed during the process to create a seamless cavity (also called lost-wax process).\n56: 2.  **Steps (must list in order):** \n57:     *   Wax pattern creation.\n58:     *   Assembly into a tree.\n59:     *   Slurry coating (dipping in refractory ceramic).\n60:     *   Stuccoing (sanding) and drying to form a shell.\n61:     *   Dewaxing (heating to melt wax out).\n62:     *   Pouring molten metal.\n63:     *   Knockout (breaking ceramic shell).\n64: 3.  **Diagram:** Draw three stages: (1) The wax tree, (2) The dipping process, (3) The final pouring of metal into the hollow ceramic shell.\n65: 4.  **Advantages:** Excellent surface finish, extremely high dimensional accuracy, no parting line, can cast complex shapes.\n66: 5.  **Limitations (Must include for 7M):** High cost, process is slow, limited to smaller castings (weight restriction).\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## CHAPTER 2: METAL JOINING\n71: \n72: ### 6. Gas Welding & 3 Flames (7 Marks)\n73: *Tier 1 - Highest Probability*\n74: **What to write for full marks:**\n75: *   **Definition:** Joining metals by melting them using an oxy-acetylene gas flame.\n76: *   **Equipment List (Add for 7M):** Oxygen cylinder (black), Acetylene cylinder (maroon), pressure regulators, hoses, welding torch.\n77: *   **Flames (MUST draw all 3):**\n78:     1.  **Neutral Flame (Equal O2 and C2H2):** \n79:         *   *Diagram:* Draw two zones: Inner luminous cone, outer envelope. \n80:         *   *Temp:* ~3200°C. \n81:         *   *Use:* Mild steel, cast iron, copper, aluminum.\n82:     2.  **Oxidizing Flame (Excess O2):** \n83:         *   *Diagram:* Draw two zones: Short, pointed inner cone, shorter outer envelope. \n84:         *   *Temp:* ~3300°C. \n85:         *   *Use:* Brass, bronze (copper-base alloys).\n86:     3.  **Carburizing/Reducing Flame (Excess C2H2):** \n87:         *   *Diagram:* Draw three zones: Inner cone, intermediate acetylene feather, outer envelope. \n88:         *   *Temp:* ~3100°C. \n89:         *   *Use:* High carbon steels, hard-facing.\n90: \n91: ### 7. Electrode Coating Purpose (3 Marks)\n92: *Easy Win*\n93: **List these 4 exact points:**\n94: 1. Provides a gaseous shield to protect molten weld pool from atmospheric oxygen and nitrogen.\n95: 2. Forms slag over the weld to protect it during cooling and refine the metal.\n96: 3. Stabilizes the arc (contains ionizing agents).\n97: 4. Adds alloying elements to the weld metal to improve properties.\n98: \n99: ### 8. Polarity in Arc Welding (3 Marks)\n100: *Easy Win*\n101: **What to write:**\n102: *   **DCSP (Direct Current Straight Polarity):** Workpiece is Positive (+), Electrode is Negative (-). 66% of heat is at the workpiece. Used for thick plates.\n103: *   **DCRP (Direct Current Reverse Polarity):** Workpiece is Negative (-), Electrode is Positive (+). 66% of heat is at the electrode. Used for thin plates (prevents burn-through) and metals with refractory oxides (Aluminum).\n104: \n105: ### 9. Brazing vs Soldering (3-4 Marks)\n106: *Easy Win*\n107: | Feature | Brazing | Soldering |\n108: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n109: | **Temperature** | Above 450°C (but below base metal melting point) | Below 450°C |\n110: | **Filler Metal** | Spelter (Copper, Zinc, Silver alloys) | Solder (Lead-Tin alloys) |\n111: | **Strength** | Stronger joint, handles higher stress | Weaker joint, handles low stress |\n112: | **Application** | Pipe fittings, carbide tool tips | Electronics, PCB boards, plumbing |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## CHAPTER 3: FORMING & SHAPING\n117: \n118: ### 10. Hot vs Cold Working (4-7 Marks)\n119: *Tier 1 & Easy Win*\n120: | Feature | Hot Working | Cold Working |\n121: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n122: | **Temperature** | Above recrystallization temperature | Below recrystallization temperature |\n123: | **Force Required** | Less force/power required | High force/power required |\n124: | **Surface Finish** | Poor surface finish (oxidation/scale) | Excellent surface finish |\n125: | **Grain Structure** | New equiaxed grains form | Grains get elongated (distorted) |\n126: | **Strength/Hardness**| Remains unchanged | Increases due to strain hardening |\n127: | **Residual Stress** | Negligible | High residual stresses |\n128: \n129: ### 11. Rolling: Derivation $l = \\sqrt{R\\Delta t}$ (7 Marks)\n130: *Tier 1 (From actual mid-sem)*\n131: **Steps for full marks:**\n132: 1.  **Diagram:** Draw two rolls (radius R), plate entering (thickness $t_0$) and leaving (thickness $t_f$). Show the contact angle $\\alpha$ and projected contact length $l$.\n133: 2.  **Draft:** Define draft $\\Delta t = t_0 - t_f$.\n134: 3.  **Geometry:** Look at the triangle formed by the roll center, the entry point, and the vertical axis.\n135: 4.  The vertical leg is $R - (\\Delta t / 2)$.\n136: 5.  The horizontal leg is $l$.\n137: 6.  The hypotenuse is $R$.\n138: 7.  **Pythagoras:** $R^2 = l^2 + (R - \\frac{\\Delta t}{2})^2$\n139: 8.  Expand: $R^2 = l^2 + R^2 - R\\Delta t + \\frac{\\Delta t^2}{4}$\n140: 9.  Cancel $R^2$. Assume $\\frac{\\Delta t^2}{4}$ is very small and ignore it.\n141: 10. Result: $0 = l^2 - R\\Delta t \\implies l^2 = R\\Delta t \\implies l = \\sqrt{R\\Delta t}$.\n142: \n143: ### 12. Rolling Numerical Formulas (7 Marks)\n144: *Tier 1 (From actual mid-sem)*\n145: **Memorize these to solve the 7-mark torque/power question:**\n146: 1.  **Draft:** $\\Delta t = h_0 - h_1$\n147: 2.  **Contact Length:** $L_c = \\sqrt{R \\cdot \\Delta t}$\n148: 3.  **True Strain:** $\\epsilon = \\ln(\\frac{h_0}{h_1})$\n149: 4.  **Average Flow Stress ($\\bar{Y}_f$):** If given directly, use it. (If given as $K\\epsilon^n$, then $\\bar{Y}_f = \\frac{K\\epsilon^n}{n+1}$).\n150: 5.  **Roll Force (F):** $F = \\bar{Y}_f \\cdot w \\cdot L_c$ (where w = width of plate).\n151: 6.  **Torque (T):** $T = F \\cdot a$ (where $a$ is the moment arm. Usually $a = 0.5 \\cdot L_c$ for hot rolling). For ONE roll.\n152: 7.  **Total Power (P):** $P = 2 \\cdot \\frac{2\\pi N T}{60}$ (Multiply by 2 because there are two rolls).\n153: \n154: ### 13. Extrusion: Forward vs Backward (7 Marks)\n155: *Tier 1*\n156: **What to write for full marks:**\n157: *   **Forward (Direct) Extrusion:** Billet is placed in a chamber. Ram pushes the billet. The metal flows in the *same* direction as the ram movement.\n158:     *   *Sketch:* Show ram pushing right, metal exiting right.\n159:     *   *Advantage:* Simpler tooling.\n160:     *   *Disadvantage:* High friction between billet and chamber walls. High force required. High scrap (butt).\n161: *   **Backward (Indirect) Extrusion:** Die is mounted on a hollow ram. Ram pushes into the billet. Metal flows in the *opposite* direction to the ram movement through the hollow ram.\n162:     *   *Sketch:* Show ram pushing right, metal exiting left (backwards) through the hollow ram.\n163:     *   *Advantage:* Zero wall friction. 25-30% less force required than forward extrusion.\n164:     *   *Disadvantage:* Mechanically complex tooling, hollow ram limits the maximum load that can be applied.\n165: \n166: ### 14. Wire vs Tube Drawing (4-7 Marks)\n167: *Tier 1*\n168: *   **Wire Drawing:** Pulling a solid metal rod through a converging die to reduce its diameter. Force is applied as tension at the exit.\n169: *   **Tube Drawing:** Reducing the diameter and wall thickness of a hollow tube.\n170: *   **Key Difference for Full Marks:** Tube drawing requires a **mandrel** inside the tube to support the internal walls and control the final internal diameter. Wire drawing has no internal support.\n171: *   **Diagrams to Draw:** \n172:     1.  Wire drawing die (solid metal passing through).\n173:     2.  Tube drawing setup showing the die AND the Mandrel.\n174: *   **Types of Mandrels (Mention for 7M):** Fixed mandrel, floating mandrel, moving mandrel.\n175: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
