ngated distorted Strength/Hardness Remains unchanged Increases due to strain hardening Residual Stress Negligible High residual stresses **11 Rolling Derivation l sqrt R Delta t 7 Marks** Tier 1 From actual mid sem Steps for full marks 1 Diagram Draw two rolls radius R plate entering thickness t 0 and leaving thickness t f Show the contact angle alpha and projected contact length l 2 Draft Define draft Delta t t 0 t f 3 Geometry Look at the triangle formed by the roll center the entry point and the vertical axis 4 The vertical leg is R Delta t / 2 5 The horizontal leg is l 6 The hypotenuse is R 7 Pythagoras R 2 l 2 R frac Delta t 2 2 8 Expand R 2 l 2 R 2 R Delta t frac Delta t 2 4 9 Cancel R 2 Assume frac Delta t 2 4 is very small and ignore it 10 Result 0 l 2 R Delta t implies l 2 R Delta t implies l sqrt R Delta t **12 Rolling Numerical Formulas 7 Marks** Tier 1 From actual mid sem Memorize these to solve the 7 mark torque/power question 1 Draft Delta t h 0 h 1 2 Contact Length L c sqrt R cdot Delta t 3 True Strain epsilon ln frac h 0 h 1 4 Average Flow Stress bar Y f If given directly use it If given as K epsilon n then bar Y f frac K epsilon n n 1 5 Roll Force F F bar Y f cdot w cdot L c where w width of plate 6 Torque T T F cdot a where a is the moment arm Usually a 0 5 cdot L c for hot rolling For ONE roll 7 Total Power P P 2 cdot frac 2 pi N T 60 Multiply by 2 because there are two rolls **13 Extrusion Forward vs Backward 7 Marks** Tier 1 What to write for full marks • Forward Direct Extrusion Billet is placed in a chamber Ram pushes the billet The metal flows in the same direction as the ram movement • Sketch Show ram pushing right metal exiting right • Advantage Simpler tooling • Disadvantage High friction between billet and chamber walls High force required High scrap butt • Backward Indirect Extrusion Die is mounted on a hollow ram Ram pushes into the billet Metal flows in the opposite direction to the ram movement through the hollow ram • Sketch Show ram pushing right metal exiting left backwards through the hollow ram • Advantage Zero wall friction 25 30 less force required than forward extrusion • Disadvantage Mechanically complex tooling hollow ram limits the maximum load that can be applied **14 Wire vs Tube Drawing 4 7 Marks** Tier 1 • Wire Drawing Pulling a solid metal rod through a converging die to reduce its diameter Force is applied as tension at the exit • Tube Drawing Reducing the diameter and wall thickness of a hollow tube • Key Difference for Full Marks Tube drawing requires a mandrel inside the tube to support the internal walls and control the final internal diameter Wire drawing has no internal support • Diagrams to Draw 1 Wire drawing die solid metal passing through 2 Tube drawing setup showing the die AND the Mandrel • Types of Mandrels Mention for 7M Fixed mandrel floating mandrel moving mandrel","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm currently focused on how best to approach the available tools. I'm prioritizing the most specific tools for each task. It's becoming clear that efficiency is key, so choosing the right tool upfront is essential for streamlined operations. My goal is to minimize wasted steps.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Selection**\n\nI'm recalling the critical instructions about tool usage and the importance of pre-planning. Given the file path, I've listed related tools to reformat the markdown content. I'm focusing on avoiding `cat`, `grep`, `ls`, and `sed` unless absolutely necessary, and I'm looking for the most specific tool for the reformatting task.\n\n\n**Evaluating Tool Efficiency**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on evaluating tool efficiency considering the lack of punctuation and constraints I need to follow It's about finding ways to reformat markdown content effectively while adhering to rules like avoiding certain tools and punctuation restrictions. The challenge is ensuring clarity without common tools or punctuation. I am also thinking about how to handle any math equation